<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#el-nino">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Niño</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as El Niño–Southern Oscillation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Niño is the periodic warming of surface waters in the central and eastern tropical Pacific, part of a climate cycle that recurs every few years. On the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> it brings torrential rains and floods to normally dry deserts and drives away fish from cold upwelling waters. Strong El Niño events have been linked to crises in ancient Andean societies, including the disruption of the Moche in the 6th century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-nino.jpg" alt="El Niño" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Content published by Rebecca Lindsey and reviewed by Tom Di Liberto. Image credit to NOAA Climate.gov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NOAA_Nino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ice cores from the Quelccaya ice cap in the southern Peruvian Andes record a severe drought between about 562 and 594, and <b>El Niño</b> rains and floods damaged canals and fields on the coast in the same period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/moche">Moche · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The oil-financed boom gave way to the debt crisis of the 1980s, and in 1998–1999 falling oil prices, <b>El Niño</b> floods and a banking collapse led the government of Jamil Mahuad to freeze bank deposits.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>…2021 study linked the collapse to a decades-long period of very heavy rain about 4,345 to 4,324 years ago, probably caused by more frequent <b>El Niño</b> conditions; flooding and poor drainage in the low-lying land may have forced the people to abandon their capital and the Taihu Plain.</blockquote><a href="/culture/liangzhu-culture">Liangzhu culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>A likely cause is an <b>El Niño</b> that brought widespread, destructive floods.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nazca-culture">Nazca culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1100 a severe drought followed by <b>El Niño</b> floods led to the burning and abandonment of Batán Grande.</blockquote><a href="/state/sican">Sicán</a></li></ul></section>
